WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:04.720
Nathan Fillion almost wasn't cast as&nbsp;
Malcolm Reynolds? Could the series&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:08.000
actually come back? Each episode cost HOW much?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:12.720
Stay tuned to find out the untold truth&nbsp;
of the short-lived classic series Firefly.

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:18.240
Firefly is a science-fiction show, concerning&nbsp;
the space-set adventures of a band of rogues,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:23.280
but it just as much keeps alive the tropes&nbsp;
of Westerns. According to "Joss Whedon:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:29.360
The Biography" by Amy Pascale, the nugget of&nbsp;
what would become Firefly came to creator Joss&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:34.720
Whedon during a vacation to London. To read on&nbsp;
the trip, he brought along the 1974 Pulitzer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:40.080
Prize-winning "The Killer Angels," a historical&nbsp;
novel about the hardscrabble lives of soldiers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:46.080
fighting in the Civil War's Battle of Gettysburg&nbsp;
in 1863. As part of a deal with the Fox network,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:50.800
he had to submit a new show soon, and the book&nbsp;
inspired him. Whedon said about the show -

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:53.840
"I wanted to play with that&nbsp;
classic notion of the frontier.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:57.760
Not the people who made history,&nbsp;
but the people history stepped on."

00:00:57.760 --> 00:01:01.120
But Whedon also wanted to&nbsp;
make a show set in space,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:05.120
offering, quote, "gritty realism&nbsp;
that wasn't an Alien ripoff," and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.120 --> 00:01:09.200
which didn't look like so many other&nbsp;
lesser made-for-TV sci-fi projects.

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:14.480
Whedon mashed all that together and it became&nbsp;
Firefly, the final touch coming when he expanded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.480 --> 00:01:20.000
the character list from just a handful to a full&nbsp;
cast of nine — just like in John Ford's classic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:25.840
1939 Western Stagecoach, which followed seven&nbsp;
strangers as they traversed the American frontier.

00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:31.760
After supporting roles in Saving Private Ryan and&nbsp;
Blast from the Past, and a big part in the '90s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:37.760
ABC sitcom Two Guys, a Girl, and a Pizza Place,&nbsp;
Nathan Fillion became a pop culture icon because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:42.480
of his time on Firefly. He's so intertwined&nbsp;
with the role of Captain Mal Reynolds for so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:48.000
many people that it's something of a surprise that&nbsp;
the part was written with another actor in mind.

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:54.320
At a 2014 Comic-Con, Nicholas Brendon, best&nbsp;
known as Xander on Buffy the Vampire Slayer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.320 --> 00:01:58.480
said that his boss, creator Joss Whedon,&nbsp;
approached him in his trailer and offered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.480 --> 00:02:03.760
him the role of a spacecraft captain in the new&nbsp;
show he was writing. Whedon planned on starting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:10.080
the show after "Buffy" wrapped production in 2003,&nbsp;
but then Fox wanted the show six months earlier&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:15.920
than originally planned, meaning Brendon couldn't&nbsp;
do Firefly because his old job remained ongoing.

00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:19.360
According to Whedon's biography,&nbsp;
Neil Patrick Harris auditioned for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:23.280
the role of Simon Tam and Rebecca&nbsp;
Gayheart shot scenes as Companion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:27.360
Inara before leaving the series and&nbsp;
being replaced by Morena Baccarin.

00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:33.200
While it would eventually be hailed as one of&nbsp;
the best shows of the 2000s and ranked among TV's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:38.640
all-time greatest science-fiction works, Firefly&nbsp;
was phenomenally unpopular when it debuted on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:46.080
Fox's primetime schedule in the fall of 2002. It&nbsp;
was officially listed as the 125th most-watched&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:53.440
show out of 156 shows in the 2002-2003 season,&nbsp;
although Fox didn't do the series any favors.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:59.440
Of 14 produced episodes, only 11 aired, and when&nbsp;
they did broadcast, they were presented out of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.440 --> 00:03:05.760
order. Firefly aired on Fox on Friday nights in&nbsp;
a time slot where shows often died from neglect,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:10.640
and it's clear that the network didn't know&nbsp;
what to make of the show or how to market it.

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:15.680
According to Insider, executives thought the&nbsp;
show was too dark, but also wanted more gunplay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:20.240
and lamented that no romance was possible&nbsp;
between Captain Mal Reynolds and crew&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.240 --> 00:03:25.040
member Zoe Washburne on account of how&nbsp;
the latter was married to pilot Wash.

00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:28.480
"What if we just told Mal we need a&nbsp;
couple days 'stead of asking him?"

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:31.360
"He's the captain Wash."

00:03:31.360 --> 00:03:32.800
"Right - I'm just the husband."

00:03:33.440 --> 00:03:37.600
It seems as if the few viewers Firefly&nbsp;
actually attracted during its ill-fated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.600 --> 00:03:41.120
original run on Fox in 2002 became devoted fans of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:45.360
the show who dedicated themselves to&nbsp;
making sure the series continued on.

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:49.360
Accurately predicting that the show probably&nbsp;
wasn't going to stick around for long without&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.360 --> 00:03:54.480
some kind of intervention, viewers connected via&nbsp;
message boards, email forwards, and the fansite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.480 --> 00:03:58.960
Immediate Assistance to save Firefly&nbsp;
before it was even officially canceled.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.960 --> 00:04:04.800
Between October and December 2002, Firefly&nbsp;
fans mailed thousands of postcards both to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:09.840
Fox and the show's sponsors to thank them&nbsp;
for airing a show of such merit and quality.

00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:16.240
But Fox couldn't be swayed from its decision to&nbsp;
discontinue Firefly in late 2002. Undeterred,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.240 --> 00:04:21.120
creator Joss Whedon shopped the show around, but&nbsp;
no other channel had any interest in scooping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:27.360
up Firefly. Three years later, robust DVD box set&nbsp;
sales in the range of 500,000 convinced Universal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:33.840
Pictures to spend $40 million on a Firefly sequel&nbsp;
movie — Serenity hit theaters in fall 2005.

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:38.000
"We can't die Bendis. And you&nbsp;
know why? Because we are so very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.560 --> 00:04:42.400
pretty. We are just too&nbsp;
pretty for God to let us die."

00:04:43.760 --> 00:04:49.120
With such an open-ended concept, Firefly could&nbsp;
have generated dozens of episodes over many years.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.120 --> 00:04:53.280
Unfortunately, it was canceled halfway&nbsp;
through its first and only season,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.280 --> 00:04:57.840
leaving many potential and pitched episodes&nbsp;
left permanently on the "what if?" pile.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.840 --> 00:05:02.400
During a cast panel at 2012's New York&nbsp;
Comic-Con, cast member Nathan Fillion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:07.600
remembered costar Alan Tudyk's voluminous&nbsp;
and detailed episode ideas, explaining -

00:05:07.600 --> 00:05:11.680
"He had a great one where there were some&nbsp;
criminals who engage in illegal dogfights,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:15.840
and there was a planet where one side&nbsp;
of the planet was perpetual night.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:18.640
They had these big, feral dogs there."

00:05:18.640 --> 00:05:23.680
"Dead or Alive" would've been the 15th episode of&nbsp;
Firefly, and its script was completely written,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.680 --> 00:05:28.240
but ultimately unused, except for some&nbsp;
parts pushed into the episode "The Message."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.240 --> 00:05:32.320
It involves a factory bombing near where&nbsp;
the Serenity crew attends a fair, and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.320 --> 00:05:38.160
suspect is an old friend of Mal and Zoe. A future&nbsp;
episode also would've explained Inara's syringe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.160 --> 00:05:42.400
that makes an enigmatic appearance in the first&nbsp;
episode of "Firefly." According to writer and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.400 --> 00:05:48.560
producer Tim Minear in the 2012 TV special&nbsp;
"Browncoats Unite," it was a "magic syringe" that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.560 --> 00:05:53.680
upon injection, killed instantly anyone&nbsp;
that assaulted Inara. The Companion was set&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.680 --> 00:05:58.240
to endure a kidnapping that would end in the&nbsp;
deaths of her assailants, a gang of Reavers.

00:05:59.040 --> 00:06:02.800
TV shows cost a lot of money to make,&nbsp;
particularly ones set in the future,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.800 --> 00:06:08.240
where the action takes place primarily on a&nbsp;
spaceship and on a new planet each episode.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.240 --> 00:06:13.360
Firefly was thus an expensive series, with&nbsp;
each episode racking up about $2 million in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.360 --> 00:06:18.160
production costs, according to Slashdot. But even&nbsp;
with that high of a budget, the show's crew still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.160 --> 00:06:23.040
had to find ways to scrimp, save, and scale back&nbsp;
in order to make the show they wanted to make.

00:06:23.680 --> 00:06:29.440
For example, according to Recycled Movie Costumes,&nbsp;
enemy Alliance soldiers on Firefly wore the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.440 --> 00:06:35.520
armored uniforms used in Starship Troopers and&nbsp;
on an episode of Power Rangers. And as Firefly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.520 --> 00:06:41.040
is at its heart a Western, the show finds Captain&nbsp;
Mal Reynolds on various planets, riding a horse.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.040 --> 00:06:45.600
According to Fillion on Twitter, the&nbsp;
production used the same horse every time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.600 --> 00:06:49.520
Michael K. Johnson writes in "Hoo-Doo&nbsp;
Cowboys and Bronze Buckaroos" that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.520 --> 00:06:53.920
Firefly reused props from another Fox&nbsp;
revisionist Western, and two of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.920 --> 00:06:59.200
guns used were leftovers from the early '90s&nbsp;
show "The Adventures of Brisco County, Jr."

00:07:00.080 --> 00:07:05.520
Firefly is named after its central setting:&nbsp;
a "firefly-class" spaceship named "Serenity."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.520 --> 00:07:08.640
A lot of thought and effort went into&nbsp;
making sure it was the perfect ship for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.640 --> 00:07:13.280
the series and the characters who inhabited&nbsp;
it. Production designer Carey Meyer told MTV&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.280 --> 00:07:16.800
News about his discussions with Joss&nbsp;
Whedon regarding the ship, saying -

00:07:16.800 --> 00:07:20.160
"He wanted each of those spaces&nbsp;
to have a distinct identity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.160 --> 00:07:23.840
so that you always knew where you were,&nbsp;
no matter where you were in the ship."

00:07:23.840 --> 00:07:27.680
After the show ended production&nbsp;
during the 2002-2003 season,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.680 --> 00:07:31.200
it was all disassembled rather&nbsp;
quickly with Meyer saying -

00:07:31.200 --> 00:07:33.520
"It's very difficult to hold&nbsp;
on to that sort of thing."

00:07:33.520 --> 00:07:37.280
He went on to say that the wood used&nbsp;
for the ship's set was recycled into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.280 --> 00:07:41.600
his next gig as production manager&nbsp;
on the short-lived CBS drama "Vegas."

00:07:42.400 --> 00:07:47.680
About six years after the demise of Firefly,&nbsp;
Nathan Fillion found his next big project with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.680 --> 00:07:53.600
ABC's Castle, portraying the title character,&nbsp;
a mystery novelist who helps the NYPD solve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.600 --> 00:07:59.680
crimes as research. While Castle would run for&nbsp;
eight seasons and make Fillion very famous,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.680 --> 00:08:04.560
Firefly was never far from his heart,&nbsp;
as he told Entertainment Weekly in 2011:

00:08:04.560 --> 00:08:07.920
"It was my favorite job ever.&nbsp;
What wasn't great about it?"

00:08:07.920 --> 00:08:11.040
Nor were Fillion's Firefly&nbsp;
co-stars out of his life forever.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.040 --> 00:08:15.200
A full half of Fillion's shipmates popped&nbsp;
up on Castle over the course of its run.

00:08:15.200 --> 00:08:20.240
Further, Castle is loaded with references,&nbsp;
big and small, to Fillion's previous series.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.240 --> 00:08:24.560
For example, in the 2009 Halloween&nbsp;
episode "Vampire Weekend," Castle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.560 --> 00:08:29.440
tries on his costume — a long brown coat,&nbsp;
much like the one he wore as Captain Mal.

00:08:29.440 --> 00:08:30.720
"What exactly are you supposed to be?"

00:08:32.080 --> 00:08:32.880
"Space cowboy."

00:08:33.600 --> 00:08:38.320
"Ok, A - there are no cows in space. B -&nbsp;
Didn't you wear that like, five years ago?"
 &nbsp;

00:08:38.320 --> 00:08:41.760
A moment where Castle takes down&nbsp;
an assailant with a single gunshot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.760 --> 00:08:46.080
mirrors a similar sequence in "Firefly"&nbsp;
when Jayne shoots an armed attacker in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.080 --> 00:08:49.920
leg while aiming for the attacker's&nbsp;
head, which Castle also admits to.

00:08:50.720 --> 00:08:55.360
In the safe, Alliance-controlled Central&nbsp;
planets or core worlds in the Firefly universe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.360 --> 00:09:00.480
Reavers are akin to the boogeyman; those that&nbsp;
hang around in less friendly environments know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.480 --> 00:09:05.600
that they're real, dangerous, and terrifying.&nbsp;
Reavers are cannibalistic pirates, brutalizing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.600 --> 00:09:10.480
their victims in any number of ways before&nbsp;
stealing their cargo, rarely leaving survivors.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.480 --> 00:09:15.040
They're a recurring menace in the sole season&nbsp;
of Firefly and the spinoff Serenity movie.

00:09:15.040 --> 00:09:19.520
At a panel at WonderCon 2005, creator Joss&nbsp;
Whedon explained that he came up with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.520 --> 00:09:24.400
Reavers as a parallel to the perceived villains&nbsp;
of old Western movies and fiction, saying -

00:09:24.400 --> 00:09:28.560
"Every story needs a monster. In the stories&nbsp;
of the Old West, it was the Apaches."

00:09:28.560 --> 00:09:33.680
In Western movies, white settlers were usually the&nbsp;
so-called good guys and the Apache were demonized&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.680 --> 00:09:38.320
as the de facto bad guys. Whedon didn't want&nbsp;
the rivalry to have anything to do with race,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.320 --> 00:09:42.560
however, preferring to imply that the&nbsp;
Reavers became the way they are because,

00:09:42.560 --> 00:09:46.720
"Anyone who goes out into space&nbsp;
and goes mad can become a monster."

00:09:47.360 --> 00:09:52.080
In March 2011, Firefly received some new&nbsp;
attention when the Science Channel acquired&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.080 --> 00:09:57.440
rebroadcast rights to the sci-fi series&nbsp;
and began airing reruns of all 14 episodes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.440 --> 00:10:02.320
In an interview relating to that news, and asked&nbsp;
by Entertainment Weekly if he'd return to Firefly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.320 --> 00:10:04.720
Fillion replied in the affirmative, saying -

00:10:04.720 --> 00:10:09.280
"If I got $300 million from the California&nbsp;
Lottery, the first thing I would do is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.280 --> 00:10:13.520
buy the rights to Firefly, make it on my&nbsp;
own, and distribute it on the internet."

00:10:13.520 --> 00:10:17.040
Legions of Firefly fans took Fillion&nbsp;
at his word and teamed up to actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.040 --> 00:10:20.480
acquire the rights to the show and hand&nbsp;
them over in the hopes that production&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.480 --> 00:10:25.680
of new episodes could begin. The website&nbsp;
HelpNathanBuyFirefly.com was established,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.680 --> 00:10:31.440
and about 70,000 fans pledged to donate money&nbsp;
to the cause. This project ultimately didn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.440 --> 00:10:36.400
pan out — rights-holder 20th Century Fox just&nbsp;
wasn't looking to sell, no matter the price.

00:10:37.280 --> 00:10:43.280
The popularity of Firefly has only grown since&nbsp;
its cancellation in 2002, and there just might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.280 --> 00:10:48.800
be a market for a revival of the series. At the&nbsp;
2020 Television Critics Association press tour,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.800 --> 00:10:53.040
Fox's president of entertainment Michael&nbsp;
Thorn floated the idea, telling TheWrap -

00:10:53.040 --> 00:10:56.800
"The macro answer is, any time we&nbsp;
look at one of our classic titles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.800 --> 00:11:02.240
if there's a way to reinvent it for today&nbsp;
so it's as resonant now as the original was,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.240 --> 00:11:04.960
and is, to the fans, we're wide open."

00:11:04.960 --> 00:11:09.440
He added that a few years prior, during the&nbsp;
reboot craze that brought the likes of Twin Peaks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.440 --> 00:11:15.360
Roseanne, and The X-Files back to TV, a Firefly&nbsp;
continuation was considered but he also revealed -

00:11:15.360 --> 00:11:18.880
"But we had The Orville on the air and&nbsp;
it didn't make sense for us to have,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.880 --> 00:11:24.000
as a broadcast network who is very targeted,&nbsp;
to have two space franchises on our air."

00:11:24.000 --> 00:11:27.120
Firefly executive producer&nbsp;
Tim Minear further mentioned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.120 --> 00:11:30.320
that he and creator Joss Whedon&nbsp;
discussed a reboot, saying -

00:11:30.320 --> 00:11:34.160
"We have talked about different permutations&nbsp;
and how that might work. [...] I would love&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.160 --> 00:11:38.560
to see, like, an eight- or 10-episode&nbsp;
limited adventure in that universe."

